M="biochem-metab-3"
SUB="Metabolism"
T={
"gluconeo":[("Which enzyme is absent from skeletal muscle, preventing it from releasing glucose?",["Glucose-6-phosphatase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Glycogen phosphorylase","Hexokinase"],0,"Muscle glycogen serves only the muscle; the liver and kidney have the phosphatase."),
 ("Pyruvate carboxylase requires which cofactor and allosteric activator?",["Biotin and acetyl-CoA","Thiamine and NADH","Cobalamin and ATP","Pyridoxine and citrate"],0,"Rising acetyl-CoA during fasting is the signal to divert pyruvate toward glucose."),
 ("Why can even-chain fatty acids not be converted to glucose?",["The pyruvate dehydrogenase step is irreversible","Acetyl-CoA cannot enter the mitochondrion","Beta-oxidation consumes all the NADH","Fatty acids cannot cross into the liver"],0,"Acetyl-CoA cannot be turned back into pyruvate. Glycerol and odd-chain fatty acids can make glucose because they enter past that block.",3)],
"hmp":[("The HMP shunt produces which two key products?",["NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate","ATP and NADH for the chain","FADH2 and pyruvate for TCA","Acetyl-CoA and carbon dioxide"],0,"It neither makes nor consumes ATP."),
 ("Transketolase in the non-oxidative phase requires:",["Thiamine pyrophosphate","Biotin","Lipoic acid","Pyridoxal phosphate"],0,"Its activity is used as a laboratory measure of thiamine status."),
 ("Beside reductive biosynthesis, NADPH is essential in neutrophils for:",["The respiratory burst","Chemotaxis toward bacteria","Phagosome-lysosome fusion","Antibody opsonisation"],0,"NADPH oxidase uses it to generate superoxide, which is why chronic granulomatous disease impairs killing.",2)],
"g6pd":[("G6PD deficiency is inherited as:",["X-linked recessive","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant","Mitochondrial"],0,"It is common in Thailand and other historically malarious regions."),
 ("Which red cell finding is characteristic?",["Heinz bodies and bite cells","Howell-Jolly bodies after splenectomy","Coarse basophilic stippling","Target cells on the film"],0,"Oxidised haemoglobin precipitates as Heinz bodies, and the spleen removes them leaving bite cells."),
 ("A boy is tested for G6PD during an acute haemolytic episode and the level is reported as normal. What is the best explanation?",["The most deficient cells have already lysed","The assay is unreliable during a fever","Haemolysis raises G6PD levels transiently","He must have a different enzyme defect"],0,"Reticulocytes have higher enzyme activity, so testing during haemolysis can be falsely normal. Repeat it weeks later.",3)],
"glycogen":[("The debranching enzyme has how many catalytic activities?",["Two","One","Three","Four"],0,"It transfers a three-residue chain and then hydrolyses the alpha-1,6 bond."),
 ("Branch points in glycogen are linked by:",["Alpha-1,6 bonds","Alpha-1,4 bonds","Beta-1,4 bonds","Beta-1,6 bonds"],0,"The straight chains use alpha-1,4; branching every 8 to 12 residues allows rapid simultaneous degradation."),
 ("Why does branching matter physiologically?",["It creates many ends to cleave at once","It makes the glycogen granule less soluble","It protects glycogen from lysosomal enzymes","It allows storage without binding water"],0,"More non-reducing ends means glucose can be mobilised far faster during exercise or hypoglycaemia.",2)],
"gsd":[("Which glycogen storage disease is also a lysosomal storage disease?",["Pompe","Von Gierke","McArdle","Cori"],0,"Acid maltase is the lysosomal alpha-1,4-glucosidase, so Pompe belongs to both groups."),
 ("A child has exercise-induced cramps and dark urine but entirely normal blood glucose. Which enzyme is deficient?",["Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","Hepatic glucose-6-phosphatase","The debranching enzyme","The branching enzyme"],0,"McArdle disease affects muscle only, which is why blood glucose is untouched."),
 ("An infant has massive hepatomegaly, severe fasting hypoglycaemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricaemia and hyperlipidaemia. Which enzyme defect explains all of these at once?",["Glucose-6-phosphatase","Acid maltase","Muscle phosphorylase","Branching enzyme"],0,"Von Gierke blocks the final shared step of glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, so glucose cannot leave the liver and the accumulating substrate spills into lactate, urate and lipid.",3)],
"fructose":[("Deficiency of fructokinase causes:",["A benign condition, fructose in the urine","Severe liver failure in infancy","Cataracts within the first year","Progressive renal failure in childhood"],0,"Essential fructosuria is harmless because fructose is never trapped as a phosphate ester."),
 ("Hereditary fructose intolerance is caused by deficiency of:",["Aldolase B","Fructokinase","Sucrase","Aldose reductase"],0,"Fructose-1-phosphate accumulates and sequesters inorganic phosphate."),
 ("Why does hereditary fructose intolerance cause hypoglycaemia?",["Trapped phosphate blocks hepatic glucose output","Fructose directly stimulates insulin release","Aldolase B is also needed for glucose uptake","Fructose inhibits intestinal glucose absorption"],0,"Phosphate is sequestered in fructose-1-phosphate, so the liver cannot make or release glucose. Symptoms begin when fruit or sucrose is introduced.",3)],
"galactose":[("Isolated cataracts in an infant with no liver disease suggest deficiency of:",["Galactokinase","Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase","Aldolase B","Hexosaminidase A"],0,"Galactitol accumulates in the lens. Classic galactosaemia adds liver failure and intellectual disability."),
 ("Classic galactosaemia results from deficiency of:",["Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase","Galactokinase, the first enzyme of the path","Lactase in the intestinal brush border","UDP-galactose 4-epimerase in the liver"],0,"Symptoms begin with milk feeding, since lactose is the main dietary source."),
 ("Infants with classic galactosaemia are particularly prone to sepsis with which organism?",["Escherichia coli","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Listeria monocytogenes","Staphylococcus aureus"],0,"E. coli sepsis is a recognised and early complication of classic galactosaemia.",2)],
"sorbitol":[("Tissues damaged by the polyol pathway share which feature?",["Aldose reductase without sorbitol dehydrogenase","Both of the polyol enzymes are absent","Both enzymes are present in excess","No glucose transporters in the membrane"],0,"Sorbitol is made but cannot be cleared, so it accumulates osmotically in lens, retina, kidney and nerve."),
 ("Aldose reductase consumes which cofactor?",["NADPH","NADH","FADH2","ATP"],0,"This also depletes the NADPH available for glutathione recycling, adding oxidative stress."),
 ("The mechanism of cataract formation in galactosaemia is most similar to which diabetic complication?",["Sorbitol-induced lens swelling","Glycation of haemoglobin","Basement membrane thickening","Macrophage foam cell formation"],0,"Galactitol and sorbitol are both trapped polyols that draw water into the lens.",2)],
"sphingo":[("Which finding distinguishes Tay-Sachs from Niemann-Pick disease?",["Absence of splenomegaly","Presence of a cherry-red macula","Autosomal recessive inheritance","Onset in infancy"],0,"Both have a cherry-red macula, but the spleen is spared in Tay-Sachs."),
 ("Which two lysosomal storage diseases are X-linked recessive?",["Fabry and Hunter","Gaucher and Hurler","Tay-Sachs and Krabbe","Niemann-Pick and Fabry"],0,"All the others in these groups are autosomal recessive."),
 ("A man has episodic burning pain in his hands and feet, clusters of dark red skin papules, and progressive renal failure. Which enzyme is deficient?",["Alpha-galactosidase A","Glucocerebrosidase","Hexosaminidase A","Arylsulfatase A"],0,"Fabry disease: ceramide trihexoside accumulates in vascular endothelium, producing acroparaesthesia, angiokeratomas and renal and cardiac disease.",3)],
"muco":[("Which feature separates Hunter syndrome from Hurler syndrome?",["Clear corneas","Coarse facial features","Joint stiffness","Hepatosplenomegaly"],0,"Hurler clouds the cornea; Hunter does not, and Hunter is milder and X-linked."),
 ("Hurler syndrome is caused by deficiency of:",["Alpha-L-iduronidase","Iduronate-2-sulfatase","Hexosaminidase A","Sphingomyelinase"],0,"Hunter is due to iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency instead."),
 ("Mucopolysaccharidoses differ from sphingolipidoses mainly in that the stored material is:",["Glycosaminoglycan","Sphingomyelin","Ganglioside","Cerebroside sulfate"],0,"Storing glycosaminoglycans affects bone, joints and connective tissue rather than mainly the nervous system.",2)],
}
